<DocsLayout title="Data & curation" description="Supported dataset formats and the curation-first workflow in super-charge-ml." current="data">
  <p class="eyebrow"><span></span> Inputs and evidence</p>
  <h1>Meet the data<br /><em>where it is.</em></h1>
  <p class="lead">The framework accepts common files, in-memory tables, and bounded streams. The input contract is broad; the curation contract is strict.</p>

  <h2>Supported inputs</h2>
  <div class="doc-cards">
    <div class="doc-card"><small>FILES</small><h3>Open tabular formats</h3><p>CSV, TSV, TAB, JSON records, JSONL / NDJSON, Parquet, Feather, Arrow, XLSX, and XLSM. Local-file resume checkpoints bind to the raw-byte SHA-256 and reject a changed snapshot.</p></div>
    <div class="doc-card"><small>IN MEMORY</small><h3>Table interchange</h3><p>pandas DataFrame, PyArrow Table, Polars DataFrame or LazyFrame, and Arrow stream protocol objects.</p></div>
    <div class="doc-card"><small>STREAMING</small><h3>Large inputs</h3><p>Chunked CSV and newline-delimited JSON can be profiled without loading every row into memory.</p></div>
    <div class="doc-card"><small>INCREMENTAL</small><h3>Event semantics</h3><p>Event IDs, ordered checkpoints, watermarks, duplicate classification, late-data policy, atomic commit state, and an idempotent sink boundary are transport-neutral. <code>EventConsumer</code> stages broker acknowledgements durably after the sink commit and retries them after a restart. It does not claim distributed exactly-once delivery.</p></div>
    <div class="doc-card"><small>WAREHOUSE</small><h3>Query engines</h3><p><code>WarehouseConnector</code> gives warehouse and lakehouse engines one bounded reader contract. Inject a schema callback and a paged batch callback; the connector owns query-bound identity, retries, row limits, schema validation, and resumable checkpoints without hiding a cloud SDK.</p></div>
    <div class="doc-card"><small>EVENT STREAMS</small><h3>Bounded broker polling</h3><p><code>EventStreamSource</code> accepts a caller-owned poll callback for Kafka-, Pulsar-, Kinesis-, Pub/Sub-, Event Hubs-, NATS-, and Redpanda-style URI boundaries. Pair it with <code>EventConsumer</code> to bind a consumer group, retry reads, reject oversized batches, persist pending acknowledgements, and keep the broker SDK at the transport edge.</p></div>
    <div class="doc-card"><small>RANGE READS</small><h3>Bounded object scans</h3><p>Range-backed JSONL and NDJSON sources read only the requested byte windows and resume from byte checkpoints. Record-size limits, invalid JSON, and in-object schema changes fail with structured errors; arbitrary binary formats still require an explicit decoder or streaming adapter.</p></div>
    <div class="doc-card"><small>OBJECT BYTES</small><h3>Bring a versioned decoder</h3><p><code>ObjectStoreConnector</code> accepts bounded bytes from any object store and a caller-owned decoder that returns a DataFrame. The decoder name and version become part of source identity, so checkpoints cannot mix decoding semantics.</p></div>
    <div class="doc-card"><small>DOCUMENTS</small><h3>Curate before NLP</h3><p>Document collections preserve content hashes, page and section provenance, bounded language profiles, duplicate groups, label composition, and conflicting-label findings before a tokenizer or model is chosen.</p></div>
    <div class="doc-card"><small>EXTENSION</small><h3>Custom readers</h3><p>Register a <code>TableReader</code> when your source needs a domain-specific parser before it becomes a table.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Declare before you ingest</h2>
  <p>The modality is a semantic contract, not a file-extension guess. Set it explicitly when constructing <code>MLProject</code> or <code>DataSpec</code>. The manifest carries that declaration into planning; a non-tabular declaration cannot silently inherit the tabular beta path.</p>
  <pre><code>project = MLProject(
    data="events.parquet",
    target="label",
    modality="tabular",
)</code></pre>

  <h2>The curation pass</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Identify:</strong> fingerprint the schema, rows, target, and source.</li>
    <li><strong>Profile:</strong> inspect missingness, duplicates, label quality, representativeness, and feature roles.</li>
    <li><strong>Protect:</strong> detect leakage and choose a validation strategy that matches time, groups, treatment, or events.</li>
    <li><strong>Decide:</strong> record what was kept, repaired, excluded, or deferred in <code>curation_report.json</code>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Consume an event stream safely</h2>
  <p>The consumer contract separates three responsibilities. The source polls a bounded batch. Your writer commits unseen events to the downstream sink with event IDs as idempotency keys. Your broker adapter acknowledges only after that commit is durable. If acknowledgement delivery fails, the ledger keeps a JSON sidecar and retries it before polling the next batch; the sink is not run again.</p>
  <pre><code>{`source = EventStreamSource("kafka://cluster/orders", poll)
ledger = IncrementalIngestionLedger("state/orders.json", source.source_identity)
consumer = EventConsumer(
    source,
    ledger,
    consumer_group="orders-worker",
    writer=write_idempotently,
    acknowledge=broker_acknowledge,
)
evidence = consumer.consume(limit=500)
print(evidence.to_dict())`}</code></pre>
  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Important boundary:</strong> the core package proves durable local ordering and retry semantics. A real Kafka/Pulsar/Kinesis adapter still owns leases, partition assignment, broker transactions, credentials, and the independent evidence needed for distributed exactly-once claims.</p></div>

  <h2>Decode an object format you own</h2>
  <p>The built-in object decoder handles CSV, JSONL, and Parquet. For a domain-specific format, inject the transport and the decoder together. The decoder is deliberately required to return a bounded <code>pandas.DataFrame</code>; it owns parsing, schema interpretation, decompression, validation, and any format-specific security policy.</p>
  <pre><code>{`from io import BytesIO

import pandas as pd

from scml.data.readers import ObjectStoreConnector


def decode_custom(uri: str, payload: bytes) -> pd.DataFrame:
    # The format owner decides how these bytes become rows.
    return pd.read_json(BytesIO(payload), lines=True)


connector = ObjectStoreConnector(
    "s3://bucket/events.custom",
    read_bytes=object_store.read,
    decoder=decode_custom,
    decoder_name="events-jsonl-v2",
    max_bytes=256 * 1024 * 1024,
)
batch = connector.read_rows(limit=10_000)
next_batch = connector.read_rows(batch.checkpoint, limit=10_000)`}</code></pre>
  <p>A custom decoder does not create a production capability by itself. Keep its dependency lock, license, parser limits, raw-byte identity, malformed-input tests, and resource evidence with the dataset run. Bounded object batches expose the raw payload SHA-256 and bind it into the resume checkpoint; if the object changes before the next batch, the connector fails closed instead of mixing snapshots. This connector is bounded in-memory ingestion; use a format-specific range or streaming adapter when the object cannot safely fit in memory.</p>

  <h2>What to review before modelling</h2>
  <p>Do not jump from a successful parse to a model score. Review source and profiled row counts, feature decisions, cleaning notes, quality findings, risk findings, duplicate-content label conflicts, and dropped columns. A curation finding is an input to the modelling decision—not noise around it. Blocking findings stop the run until a reviewer resolves, waives, or marks them false-positive.</p>
  <h2>The dataset card</h2>
  <p>Tabular packaged runs write <code>dataset_card.json</code>. Experimental modality lifecycles carry the equivalent card inside their documentation evidence. In both cases it is the human-facing summary of the snapshot: intended use, explicit exclusions, composition, source ownership, lineage, privacy controls, limitations, and findings that still need review. Its default status is <code>machine_generated_unreviewed</code>; that is deliberate. A generated card is context, not approval.</p>
  <pre><code>{`{
  "intended_use": "Training and evaluation for binary classification...",
  "out_of_scope": ["It is not a release approval..."],
  "review_status": "machine_generated_unreviewed",
  "composition": {"source_rows": 12000, "profiled_rows": 12000}
}`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Bind non-tabular governance before modelling</h2>
  <p>Non-tabular lifecycles accept a typed <code>ModalityManifest</code>. It binds the exact content hash and sample count to the modality, task, source ownership, transformation and label lineage, privacy policy, and curation findings. The lifecycle rejects a manifest that does not match the adapter before it starts training.</p>
  <pre><code>{`from scml.contracts.data import ModalityManifest, PrivacyMetadata, SourceMetadata

manifest = ModalityManifest(
    dataset_id="documents-2026-10-21",
    snapshot_id="approved-export-17",
    modality="document",
    task="binary_classification",
    sample_count=len(dataset.samples),
    content_hash=dataset.content_hash,
    source=SourceMetadata(
        owner="records-team",
        acquisition_method="approved export",
        license="internal-use",
    ),
    privacy=PrivacyMetadata(
        classification="restricted",
        contains_pii=True,
        consent_basis="employment-contract",
        retention_period="90d",
        deletion_policy="case-closure",
    ),
)

report = lifecycle.run(adapter, package_path, signing_key, manifest=manifest)`}</code></pre>
  <p>The manifest is evidence, not approval. A reviewer still needs to inspect the source, consent, retention, label decisions, and open findings. If metadata is omitted, the generated card says so explicitly instead of implying that provenance or privacy was assessed.</p>

  <h2>Verify a modality package before prediction</h2>
  <p>The experimental lifecycle exposes a library predictor for callers that already own a typed adapter. It verifies the signed package, modality, feature names, and estimator dimension before converting new samples into features. Codec choice and raw transport remain caller-owned.</p>
  <pre><code>{`from scml.models.modality_lifecycle import ModalityPredictor

predictor = ModalityPredictor.from_package(
    adapter, "artifacts/modality-package", signing_key
)
predictions = predictor.predict(new_samples)
probabilities = predictor.predict_proba(new_samples)
print(predictor.health())`}</code></pre>
  <p>This is a checked library inference surface, not an HTTP server or a claim that the representation is production-ready. Reference serving, external resource-fault evidence, and independent release review remain open for experimental modalities.</p>

  <h2>Data boundaries beyond tables</h2>
  <p>The repository now has dependency-light foundations for documents, images, graphs, audio, video, and geospatial vectors and rasters. These foundations preserve source identity, validate the data shape, and produce bounded profiles that can feed curation, typed modality manifests, and dataset cards. Every foundation now exposes a modality curation report; documents and media flag duplicate content with conflicting labels, mixed labeled/unlabeled state, unknown language, and truncated inspection, while other modalities surface shape, encoding, duplicate, graph-structure, and CRS findings. Image ingestion also records EXIF tags and blocks release when camera, author, GPS, copyright, software, or capture-time metadata needs an explicit privacy decision. They are deliberately separate from modelling: a validated sample is not yet a supported training path.</p>
  <div class="doc-cards">
    <div class="doc-card"><small>AUDIO</small><h3>Probe before decode</h3><p>WAV metadata and bounded PCM signal statistics are read with the standard library. Other containers require an explicit caller-owned probe, so codec versions and optional dependencies remain visible in the run evidence.</p></div>
    <div class="doc-card"><small>VIDEO</small><h3>Decoder policy is yours</h3><p>Video samples require dimensions, frame timing, codec, and audio-track metadata. Use the metadata matrix by default, or provide an audited decoder for bounded, evenly sampled frame statistics. The core package never guesses from a filename or imports a codec stack implicitly.</p></div>
    <div class="doc-card"><small>GEOSPATIAL</small><h3>Coordinates need context</h3><p>GeoJSON geometry is validated before profiling; CRS is recorded explicitly. Spatial-block splitting keeps nearby features together in CRS units. Raster samples require dimensions, bands, dtype, CRS, and transform metadata; a caller-owned, named decoder may add bounded pixel summaries and measured nodata evidence.</p></div>
  </div>
  <pre><code>{`from scml.data.modalities import AudioSample, GeospatialDataset, VideoSample
from scml.data.modalities import DocumentDataset, DocumentSample

audio = AudioSample.from_bytes(
    wav_bytes, source="clip.wav", group_id="speaker-17"
)
video = VideoSample.from_bytes(encoded, video_metadata, source="clip.mp4")
places = GeospatialDataset.from_geojson(
    feature_collection, source="places.geojson", crs="EPSG:4326"
)
documents = DocumentDataset((
    DocumentSample("policy-1", "...", label="approved"),
    DocumentSample("policy-2", "...", label="review"),
))
curation = documents.curate()
assert curation.dataset_hash == documents.content_hash

audio_features = AudioDataset((audio,)).prepare_features(max_frames=100_000)
assert audio_features.frames_truncated == (False,)
audio_split = AudioDataset((audio,)).split(test_fraction=0)

# These objects can be profiled and curated. They do not silently opt into
# tabular modelling or a production support claim.`}</code></pre>
  <p>Image, document, graph, audio, video, and geospatial foundations now have a complete experimental baseline lifecycle through <code>ModalityLifecycle</code>. Raster adapters can remain metadata-only or accept a caller-owned named decoder for bounded pixel summaries, while codec selection and spatial transforms remain outside the core package. That path attaches modelling, evaluation, packaging, inference, monitoring, documentation, and recovery evidence to an explicit experimental declaration. It does not promote those modalities to supported status: the supported release boundary remains the explicitly gated tabular library path. Warehouse, lakehouse, and event-stream boundaries have tested callback contracts; broker leases, transactions, and distributed exactly-once evidence remain transport-owner responsibilities.</p>
  <h2>Modality hand-off checklist</h2>
  <p>Use the raw material and its evidence boundary to decide what the next stage is allowed to consume. A successful parser is only the first observation; the hand-off is complete when the dataset identity, curation findings, split policy, and representation limits are all persisted.</p>
  <div class="doc-cards">
    <div class="doc-card"><small>TEXT / DOCUMENTS</small><h3>Preserve spans</h3><p>Start with bytes and document boundaries. Record encoding, language, page or section spans, duplicate content, labels, and redaction decisions. A TF-IDF baseline may consume normalized text; it does not imply OCR, layout, table, or citation provenance.</p></div>
    <div class="doc-card"><small>IMAGES</small><h3>Preserve source metadata</h3><p>Start with encoded bytes and dimensions. Record channels, color space, EXIF risk, duplicate groups, label conflicts, and resize policy. A transformed-pixel baseline cannot be described as raw-image saliency or object localization.</p></div>
    <div class="doc-card"><small>AUDIO / VIDEO</small><h3>Make decoding explicit</h3><p>Start with the container and probe result. Record codec, duration, frame or sample limits, group identity, and whether a caller-owned decoder was used. No hidden codec, transcript, synchronization, or motion claim is allowed.</p></div>
    <div class="doc-card"><small>GRAPHS</small><h3>Protect topology</h3><p>Start with node and edge identity. Record direction, self-loops, components, temporal boundaries, inductive or transductive policy, and feature availability. A one-hop context report is not a graph-neural attribution.</p></div>
    <div class="doc-card"><small>GEOSPATIAL</small><h3>Keep the CRS attached</h3><p>Start with geometry bytes and CRS metadata. Validate geometry, units, joins, spatial blocks, boundary leakage, nodata, and transform policy before feature preparation. A centroid split is not a universal spatial leakage solution.</p></div>
  </div>
  <p class="meta">Hand-off rule · identity → curation → split → representation → model</p>
  <h2>Spatial split boundary</h2>
  <p><code>GeospatialDataset.prepare_features()</code> emits CRS-preserving bounds, widths, heights, centroids, and coordinate counts for a deterministic numeric hand-off. <code>GeospatialDataset.split_spatial(block_size=...)</code> requires a block size in the dataset CRS units and assigns each feature to one spatial block using its geometry centroid. Blocks, not individual features, are assigned to train, validation, and test. This prevents the common mistake of evaluating on points immediately adjacent to training points.</p>
  <p>Large polygons or lines that cross block boundaries need a domain-specific spatial join policy. The core contract does not pretend that centroid assignment solves every spatial leakage problem.</p>
  <h2>Audio feature boundary</h2>
  <p><code>AudioDataset.prepare_features()</code> produces a small, inspectable baseline matrix: amplitude mean and spread, RMS, peak level, clipping fraction, silence fraction, observed duration, PCM scale, and recorded metadata. It reads a bounded prefix and exposes <code>frames_observed</code> and <code>frames_truncated</code> beside the values. This makes resource limits and measurement scope visible in data evidence.</p>
  <p>The core implementation intentionally decodes WAV only. A sample accepted through an explicit FLAC, MP3, or other codec probe can be curated, but signal preparation stops until the caller supplies a versioned decoder adapter. There is no hidden resampling, speaker identification, transcript generation, spectrogram claim, or production audio model behind this API.</p>
  <h2>Video feature boundary</h2>
  <p><code>VideoDataset.prepare_metadata_features()</code> produces numeric evidence from the probe output: duration, dimensions, pixel count, aspect ratio, frame count and rate, audio-track count, byte size, and bytes per frame. It never decodes or samples frames, so it cannot silently choose a temporal windowing policy or claim that container metadata represents visual content.</p>
  <p>A caller-owned decoder can now provide bounded frame sampling with a pixel budget, sampled-frame provenance, and temporal frame-statistics occlusion evidence in the experimental lifecycle. Audio/video synchronization, temporal leakage controls beyond group-aware splitting, raw saliency or motion attribution, reference serving, and production video modelling remain explicit extension work. The metadata and frame-statistics matrices are curation and baseline evidence, not a production video capability.</p>
  <h2>Image privacy boundary</h2>
  <p>Image samples preserve encoded bytes for lineage, so decoding pixels does not erase metadata risk. Ingestion records the EXIF tags observed in the source and normalizes sensitive tags such as GPS, artist, camera make/model, copyright, software, and original capture time. Curation emits <code>risk.image-sensitive-metadata</code> as a blocking finding; a reviewer must redact, approve, or otherwise document the disposition before training or distribution.</p>
  <h2>Leakage-aware splitting</h2>
  <p>Image, audio, and video datasets accept an optional <code>group_id</code> such as capture session, speaker, subject, or camera. Their split methods keep each group in exactly one partition. If a caller does not provide a group, the source itself is the conservative unit; the framework will not guess identity relationships from filenames.</p>
  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Data hand-off:</strong> once curation is complete, the run carries forward the normalized schema, profile findings, selected validation strategy, and the decisions made about labels and unavailable features. The model stage consumes that contract; it does not redefine it.</p></div>
</DocsLayout>
